{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793532599,"nonce":"9PM7SUMa8Yr8uBEuzByyWG6XT+g=","title":"scalar_replace_alloc forwarded a whole stored struct to a load of part of it","message":"The pass forwards each load from a non-escaping malloc to the value last stored at the load's byte offset, keyed by offset alone. A field stored as a `{ i64, i64, f64 }` (a Python run-time number) and later read as its leading `i64` (the tag, after the load was split into scalar reads) was forwarded the whole struct. Integer instructions then took a struct operand: `le %num, 3` where `le %tag, 3` was meant.\n\nCranelift accepts it, because an aggregate value is a pointer there, and silently compares the pointer: a miscompile with no error. The LLVM tier panics in the promoter thread (`Found StructValue ... but expected the FloatValue variant`, llvm_backend.rs Le lowering) and the promotion is abandoned. A load at another offset inside a stored struct (the int or float part) read the field's Undef register instead.\n\nSeen in raytrace-simple's `Vector.reflectThrough` once `Vector.scale` and the Vector constructor were inlined into it, which happened while measuring an if-conversion pass that shrank callee bodies (since dropped). Not observed on main's own inlining choices, but nothing in the pass prevented it. Found with a temporary per-pass check that reported any Binary with a struct-typed operand; the first pass to leave one was scalar_replace_alloc.\n\nFix: a candidate is rejected unless every access at an offset has one type (pointers stand in for each other, integers of a width, structs of the same fields) and no access reaches into another offset's bytes. Test: keeps_a_field_read_in_part. zylua benchmarks neutral on the NUC (wall 0.998, peak RSS 0.998, 5 interleaved runs); zypy speed suite neutral.","files":null}]}